{"name":"Driftwood Blues","hex":"#b6c1c8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"driftwood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Floating wood that drifts with the current of a body of water.","Such a piece of wood that has been cast ashore."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/driftwood"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Driftwood","blocks":[{"text":"Driftwood is a wood that has been washed onto a shore or beach of a sea, lake, or river by the action of winds, tides or waves. It is part of beach wrack."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Bois_flott%C3%A9.jpg/330px-Bois_flott%C3%A9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Driftwood"},{"title":"Blues","blocks":[{"text":"Blues is a music genre and musical form that originated among African Americans in the Deep South of the United States around the 1860s. Blues has incorporated spirituals, work songs, field hollers, shouts, chants, and rhymed simple narrative ballads from the African-American culture. The blues form is ubiquitous in jazz, rhythm and blues, and rock and roll, and is characterized by the call-and-response pattern, the blues scale, and specific chord progressions, of which the twelve-bar blues is the most common. Blue notes, usually thirds, fifths or sevenths flattened in pitch, are also an essential part of the sound. Blues shuffles or walking bass reinforce the trance-like rhythm and form a repetitive effect known as the groove."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blues"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Beach_Driftwood_Sitges_Spain_S